592186045417] [17592186120738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cc42d44-bd5b-5eb5-9e55-ef7a7d93c31a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120739 #edb/ref 1061 19981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c27d4e1c-92bf-502f-9c02-c0bf68357dbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120761 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120761 #edb/ref 1061 25259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120761 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1383e230-aa34-5f36-a91f-c60846be8cc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120761 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121058 #edb/ref 1064 "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n![](images/b9/b91bd53b8f1807be516e2477b37fbcd9c3a7415d6cf848d84aa085bc510b5eb4.svg)\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121058 #edb/ref 1065 3519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121058 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6318f9cd-930b-5b7b-acc7-c27b5b241825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121058 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121068 #edb/ref 1064 "Note that the number of pivot columns always coincides with the number of non-zero rows in the corresponding row echelon form matrix.\nSo, technically, \n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *pivot columns* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$.\n\nOr, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *non-zero rows* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121068 #edb/ref 1065 4151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121068 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c062d466-946c-526b-aba8-0a0895671218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121068 #edb/ref 1067 "An Alternative Definition of the Rank" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319523 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319523 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319523 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "293ae16a-3b0c-55c8-8fd1-0b62cdbc95fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319840 #edb/ref 1080 "$B$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319840 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319840 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9746878c-7c14-5d13-a1ef-36f1e770e71c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319876 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a2fa24c4-cd40-5277-9097-8340a67b9342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319977 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ and $C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319977 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186319977 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bf31823e-18bc-580e-b915-028a69bb1c8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320024 #edb/ref 1080 "$C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ce6a7b23-9a34-5d28-9f7b-301a3b7c8c12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186319977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186320024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186320024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186320256 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "28e7ddf0-1cef-5068-bb9a-a3a4cbecd4b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321043 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186320256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321043 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321043 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321043 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $6?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321043 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43599508-ddd2-50d6-b640-692e29bcfb0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321043 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that a matrix of rank $6$ must have at least $6$ columns and at least $6$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $A$ since it has only $5$ columns and the matrix $B$ since it has only $4$ columns.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $6$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $C$ has exactly $6$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(C)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428422 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #ed